## 7. Specific Neuromorphic Chips

### SpiNNaker (University of Manchester)

- **SpiNNaker 1:** ~1 million ARM9 cores across 1,200 boards, 130nm CMOS
- **SpiNNaker 2:** target 10 million ARM Cortex-M4F cores, currently 5.2M cores across 720 boards, 22nm FDSOI

Each node: 18 ARM968 processor cores (1 spare), 96 kB local memory, 128 MB shared memory, packet router. Each core is 200 MHz 32-bit integer, **no floating-point hardware** (fixed-point arithmetic).

Programming model: real-time event-driven system. SC&MP (Control & Monitor Program) + SARK (Application Runtime Kernel).

### IBM TrueNorth

- **65 mW**, **5.4 billion transistors**, real-time neurosynaptic processor
- Non-von Neumann, low-power, highly-parallel, scalable, defect-tolerant
- **4,096 neurosynaptic cores** → **1 million digital neurons** + **256 million synapses**
- Event-driven routing
- Computation per core:
  1. Core receives spikes from network, stores in input buffers
  2. 1 kHz sync trigger ("tick") reads spikes, distributes across horizontal axons
  3. Where a synaptic connection exists, spike delivered to neuron through dendrite
  4. Each neuron integrates incoming spikes, updates membrane potential
  5. Leak value subtracted from membrane potential
  6. If membrane potential > threshold, spike is generated and sent into network
- Augmented integrate-and-fire neuron model (simpler than Hodgkin-Huxley or Izhikevich)
- 5 major neuron units: Leak, Leak Reversal, Synapse, Integrator, Threshold and Reset
- 6 router processes (from local, forward E/W/N/S, to local), QDI (Quasi-Delay-Insensitive) async circuits

### IBM NorthPole

Architecture + programming model for **neural inference** (not training). Built on **10 axioms**:

1. **Specialized for neural inference** — no data-dependent conditional branching, no training
2. **Biological precision** — optimized for 8-, 4-, and 2-bit low-precision
3. **Distributed modular core array** — 16×16, each core does 8192 2-bit ops/cycle, cortex-like modularity, amenable to 3D / chiplet integration
4. **Distributed memory near + intertwined with compute** — exploits data locality for energy efficiency
5. **Two dense NoCs** — gray-matter-inspired (adjacent cores, spatial computing) + white-matter-inspired (long-distance, all-cores activation redistribution)
6. **Two more NoCs** — reconfigure synaptic weights + programs on each core for high-speed compute
7. **Data-independent branching** — fully pipelined, stall-free, deterministic control. No memory misses (hallmark of von Neumann eliminated). 8 threads synchronized by construction.
8. **Co-optimized training algorithms** — low-precision constraints baked into training
9. **Codesigned software** — automatic orchestration schedule for compute/memory/comm
10. **Usage model:** write input frame → read output frame. Operates independently of attached general processor.

**Performance (ResNet50):** 0.425 GHz, 42,460 FPS, 1930 FPS per billion transistors, 4541 frames per billion cycles per billion transistors. INT8/4/2, 32 batch, 74 W. Specialized for neural inference, no off-chip memory.

### BrainScaleS-2 (Heidelberg, Human Brain Project)

| Feature | BrainScaleS-1 | BrainScaleS-2 |
|---------|---------------|---------------|
| Integration | Wafer-scale, 384 HICANNs per wafer | Single-chip ASIC |
| Neurons | Up to 196,608 per wafer | 512 per chip |
| Synapses | ~44 million per wafer | 212k–217k plastic synapses per chip |
| Architecture | Mixed-signal | Hybrid (analog core + digital processors) |
| Speed | 1,000–10,000× biological real-time | 1,000× biological real-time |
| Process | 180 nm | 65 nm |
| Plasticity | Fixed models | Programmable via PPU (custom CPU) |
| Neuron model | Adaptive spiking | **AdEx** (Adaptive Exponential Integrate-and-Fire) |

**AdEx neuron equations:**
```
C_m·V̇ = -g_l·(V - E_l) + g_l·Δ_T·exp((V - V_T)/Δ_T) - w + I
τ_w·ẇ = a·(V - E_L) - w
```

Single core: full-custom analog (synaptic crossbar + neuron circuits + analog parameter storage) + 2 digital control/plasticity processors + spike router. 4 quadrants, each with 256-row × 128-column synaptic crossbar.

### Intel Loihi (2017)

- 60 mm² in Intel 14 nm
- Fully digital but **asynchronous**
- "Unambiguous example of spike-based computation, outperforming all known conventional solutions"
- ~130,000 neurons + ~130 million synapses per chip
- 128 neuromorphic cores + 3 x86 cores
- LIF (Leaky Integrate-and-Fire) neuron model:
  ```
  a_i[t] = Σⱼ w_ij · s_j[t-1]      (synaptic input)
  u_i[t] = λ_u · u_i[t-1] + a_i[t] (current)
  v_i[t] = λ_v · v_i[t-1] + u_i[t] (voltage)
  spike when v_i > threshold
  ```
- Computational primitives: stochastic noise (for neural sampling / MCMC), configurable synaptic/axon/refractory delays (polychronous dynamics), dendritic tree processing, threshold adaptation, weight scaling

**Core microarchitecture:** SYNAPSE (incoming spikes, synaptic weights) → DENDRITE (state variables u, v) → AXON (output spikes) → mesh routing to other cores. LEARNING unit updates synaptic weights at epoch boundaries.

### Intel Loihi 2 (2021)

| | Loihi | Loihi 2 |
|--|-------|---------|
| Process | 14 nm | Intel 4 (pre-production) |
| Die size | 60 mm² | 31 mm² (half) |
| Neurons | 130K | **1M (8× more)** |
| Synapses | 130M | 120M |
| On-chip mem | 33 MB SRAM | ~25 MB |
| Neuron model | Fixed LIF | **Programmable via microcode** |
| Spike speed | Baseline | **Up to 10× faster** |
| Spike events | 1-bit binary | Up to **32-bit graded** |
| Connectivity | FPGA mediated | 10G Ethernet, GPIO, SPI |
| Software | None at launch | **Lava (open-source framework)** |

**Hala Point:** large-scale Loihi 2 system at Intel.

**New neuron models supported:** Leaky Integrate-and-Fire, Resonate-and-Fire, Resonate-and-Fire with Reset, Hopf Resonator. Hopf is complex-valued with stable limit cycle.

### LLM on Loihi 2

ICLR 2025 Workshop paper: 370M-parameter **MatMul-free** language model quantized to Loihi 2.
- 3× higher throughput, 2× less energy vs edge GPU (preliminary)
- Architecture uses **HGRN blocks** (hierarchical gated recurrent network) + MLP blocks with RMSNorm + BitLinear
- Significantly better scaling claimed

### BrainChip Akida

- First commercial neuromorphic processor (since August 2021)
- Ultra-low-power, event-based, on-chip learning
- Supports CNNs, RNNs, and custom **Temporal Event-based Neural Networks (TENNs)**
- TENNs combine spatial + temporal convolutions for spatiotemporal data
- TensorFlow-compatible MetaTF framework converts CNNs/MLPs → SNNs

### Comparison Table

| Chip | Developer | Architecture | Neurons | Synapses | Power | Notes |
|------|-----------|--------------|---------|----------|-------|-------|
| Loihi | Intel | Async digital | 130K+ | 130M | ~1000× more efficient than GPUs | On-chip learning, sparse coding |
| TrueNorth | IBM | Event-driven | 1M | 256M | 70 mW peak | Crossbar array, non-von Neumann |
| SpiNNaker | Manchester | Multi-core ARM | ~1M | Configurable | Medium | Packet-switched network |
